{"id":"h210","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-physics","question":"Suppose that the speed of an electron is 1 × 10^6 m/s and the measurementprecision involves an uncertainty in speed of 1 percent. How precisely can the position of the electronbe simultaneouslydetermined?\nIs the correct answer \"5.8 × 10^-9 m\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"5.8 × 10⁻⁹ m is the standard textbook answer from the Heisenberg uncertainty principle.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.1104},{"text":"The speed uncertainty is 1% of 1e6 m/s, i.e. 1e4 m/s.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0437},{"text":"The momentum uncertainty is mΔv ≈ 9.11e-27 kg·m/s.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0453},{"text":"Using Δx ≥ ħ/(2Δp) gives Δx ≈ 5.8e-9 m.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0976},{"text":"The question asks for position uncertainty implied by momentum uncertainty, which is what ΔxΔp ≥ ħ/2 addresses.","confidence":0.85,"lean":1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":0.1549},{"text":"The electron's speed is far below c, so nonrelativistic momentum is appropriate.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0105},{"text":"The form Δx ≥ h/(4πmΔv) gives the same result, about 5.79e-9 m.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0962},{"text":"The uncertainty principle gives only a lower bound, so 'how precisely' is answered only as a minimum.","confidence":0.5,"lean":-1,"theta":0.7854,"phi":2.3546},{"text":"Some treatments use ΔxΔp ≥ ħ, which would give about 2.9e-9 m.","confidence":0.5,"lean":-1,"theta":0.7854,"phi":-3.0663},{"text":"Textbook convention and the question's wording favor the ħ/2 form, so the answer is 5.8e-9 m.","confidence":0.85,"lean":1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":0.0756}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"